# Pin theme text-file types to LF regardless of a contributor's local
# core.autocrlf setting, and explicitly mark binary asset types as
# binary so git never attempts text normalization on them.
#
# This isn't cosmetic: scripts/compare-staging-verification.ps1
# checksums the working-tree file and compares it against Shopify's
# checksumMd5 for the deployed content. Confirmed 2026-08-11 that the
# two currently match only because core.autocrlf's CRLF conversion
# hadn't yet touched these files on this machine -- a fresh clone or a
# future checkout could silently flip working-tree files to CRLF while
# git's actual blob (and Shopify's deployed content) stays LF, breaking
# that comparison. Pinning here removes the uncertainty at the source.
*.liquid text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf

*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.png binary
*.gif binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary
*.ico binary
